  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA (thankfully) quiet night is in progress, as the environment is\ngenerally stable with no present forcing for ascent. As such,\nlow clouds and fog are developing and should plague the CWA into\nthe post-sunrise hours.\n\nThe effective outflow boundary/front remains relatively far to\nthe south and west of the region, as a result of the recent\nparade of thunderstorm complexes. The ongoing convection in\neastern IN/western OH is evidence of this boundary's position.\nExpect this general regime to continue through today and\ntonight, as HREF/CAMs continue to show the bulk of convective\nactivity more closely tied to this boundary. Still, some\nscattered showers and storms are possible during the later\nafternoon and evening, once weak capping is overcome in an\nenvironment with around 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE. North of\nPittsburgh, shear of 30-35 knots may support some storm\norganization, supporting isolated damaging wind and hail\nthreats. Any such activity should mostly fade away during the\nlater evening hours.\n\nThe heavy rain threat appears lower today, given the further\nwest favored track for convective complexes and relatively\nelongated hodographs promoting faster storm motion. HREF/REFS \n90th percentile QPF through 8 PM Thursday is an inch or lower \nfor most areas, including the area of the current Flood Watch \nthat expires at 8 AM. As such, this watch will likely be \ncancelled a few hours early.\n\nFor Thursday, severe chances appear to once again be limited, \nwith sufficient shear/instability for isolated damaging \nwind/hail threats at most. The favored zone for convective \ncomplexes should remain west of the area, but the richer \nreservoir of moisture/instability may edge closer, resulting in\nhigher rain chances to the south of Pittsburgh. This will need \nto be monitored - if the heavy rain threat in this area \nincreases, a new Flood Watch may be necessary.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThereafter the WNW flow pattern continues with nearly daily \nthunderstorm chances, although a break may be seen Saturday. \nSunday still appears to have the higher probabilities of a \nconcentrated area of precipitation given the approach of a cold \nfront. Non-zero severe weather and heavy rainfall threats will \npersist. A break in the pattern may finally arrive after that \nfront early next week as surface high pressure makes a more \ndefinitive push into the Upper Ohio Valley.",
